Rem ===================================================================
Rem mungo-caverns-say.shoddy -- rendering messages, and putting them out.
Rem 
Rem Everything the game says passes through here. Speak
Rem and SpeakFlat differ only in the leading blank line, and that
Rem difference is load-bearing: the recorded games graded it.
Rem ===================================================================

Rem ---- rendering messages ------------------------------------------------
Rem %d and %s substitute positionally; %S is the game's
Rem own invention -- it pluralizes against the *previous* %d, so one
Rem message can say "1 turn" and "4 turns" without a second template.

Rem Arguments arrive already rendered as strings: Shoddy has no way to
Rem ask a value its type, so the caller decides how each one prints and
Rem %S just asks whether the last number came out as "1".
Def ArgStr(a As Number, args As List) As String
    If a > Length(args) Then
        ""
    Else
        Nth(args, a)

Def Plural(a As Number, args As List) As String
    If a < 1 Or a > Length(args) Then
        "s"
    Else
        If Nth(args, a) = "1" Then
            ""
        Else
            "s"

Rem Walks the template once, carrying the index of the next argument and
Rem the index of the last %d seen (which is what %S looks back at).
Def RenderAt(msg As String, i As Number, n As Number, args As List,
             a As Number, lastNum As Number, acc As String) As String
    If i > n Then
        acc
    Else
        If Mid(msg, i, 1) <> "%" Then
            RenderAt(msg, i + 1, n, args, a, lastNum, acc & Mid(msg, i, 1))
        Else
            Let c = Mid(msg, i + 1, 1)
            Select Case c
                Case "d"
                    RenderAt(msg, i + 2, n, args, a + 1, a, acc & ArgStr(a, args))
                Case "s"
                    RenderAt(msg, i + 2, n, args, a + 1, lastNum, acc & ArgStr(a, args))
                Case "S"
                    RenderAt(msg, i + 2, n, args, a, lastNum, acc & Plural(lastNum, args))
                Case Else
                    RenderAt(msg, i + 1, n, args, a, lastNum, acc & "%")

Def Render(msg As String, args As List) As String
    RenderAt(msg, 1, Len(msg), args, 1, 0, "")

Rem ---- output ------------------------------------------------------------
Rem An empty message prints nothing at all, not even the blank line --
Rem several tables are sparse and rely on that.

Def Speak(msg As String)
    If Len(msg) > 0 Then
        Print("\n" & msg)

Rem pspeak(..., blank = false): the inventory list runs flush under its
Rem heading with no blank between the lines.
Def SpeakFlat(msg As String)
    If Len(msg) > 0 Then
        Print(msg)

Def SpeakId(n As Number)
    If n >= 0 Then
        Speak(Nth(Messages(), n + 1))

Def SpeakArgs(n As Number, args As List)
    If n >= 0 Then
        Speak(Render(Nth(Messages(), n + 1), args))

Rem ---- output as a value --------------------------------------------------
Rem The Speak words above print where they stand, which is why the rules
Rem cannot be tested without a console: mungo-caverns-core promises "nothing here
Rem prints or reads" and one level up that promise is broken in a hundred
Rem and sixty places.
Rem
Rem The words below say the same things as values instead. A rule that
Rem returns its output rather than emitting it is a function, and a turn
Rem built from those is Game and Command in, Game and text out -- which
Rem made the recorded games a pure-function test rather than a
Rem console-capture one.
Rem
Rem Output is a list of chunks, each exactly what Print would have been
Rem given, so Voice keeps the leading blank line and VoiceFlat does not.
Rem A rule hands its output back to its caller, and the turn loop Utters
Rem it at the flush points, so message order is preserved end to end.

Def Silent() As List Of String
    { }

Def Voice(msg As String) As List Of String
    If Len(msg) > 0 Then
        { "\n" & msg }
    Else
        { }

Def VoiceFlat(msg As String) As List Of String
    If Len(msg) > 0 Then
        { msg }
    Else
        { }

Def VoiceId(n As Number) As List Of String
    If n >= 0 Then
        Voice(Nth(Messages(), n + 1))
    Else
        { }

Def VoiceArgs(n As Number, args As List) As List Of String
    If n >= 0 Then
        Voice(Render(Nth(Messages(), n + 1), args))
    Else
        { }

Rem One utterance after another.
Def Both(a As List Of String, b As List Of String) As List Of String
    Flatten({ a, b })

Rem ---- output carried on the game ----------------------------------------
Rem The rules already thread a Game through every call, so the simplest
Rem place to accumulate what they say is on the Game itself. A rule that
Rem used to print becomes one that returns a Game with more to say, and
Rem stays a function.
Rem
Rem The game asks questions mid-turn -- for a hint, to confirm a quit, to
Rem offer a resurrection -- and each is a read between two writes, so those
Rem ten sites cannot be pure however the rest is arranged. They Flush what
Rem has been said and then read, which keeps the order exact and keeps the
Rem dialogue honestly interactive.

Def Voiced(g As Game, msg As String) As Game
    With(g, Output = Both(Output(g), Voice(msg)))

Def VoicedFlat(g As Game, msg As String) As Game
    With(g, Output = Both(Output(g), VoiceFlat(msg)))

Def VoicedId(g As Game, n As Number) As Game
    With(g, Output = Both(Output(g), VoiceId(n)))

Def VoicedArgs(g As Game, n As Number, args As List) As Game
    With(g, Output = Both(Output(g), VoiceArgs(n, args)))

Def Flushed(g As Game) As Game
    Utter(Output(g))
    With(g, Output = Silent())

Rem The one place output becomes an effect.
Def Utter(out As List Of String)
    UtterFrom(out, 1, Length(out))

Def UtterFrom(out As List Of String, i As Number, n As Number)
    If i <= n Then
        Print(Nth(out, i))
        UtterFrom(out, i + 1, n)

